WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.620
Firstly, many of us would like to thank you for all of your acknowledgements, good energy, and your support.

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:11.360
We thank you to all the sovereignty you have borne for us.

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:15.640
Like you said we ask for altura rose.

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:18.880
Thank you all very much for having me on this vlog.

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:23.620
This has been the final walk of this journey with all of you.

00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:26.140
As usual, I will see you in the next one.

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:40.620
For me, as for countless others, the natural world is the greatest of all treasures. And

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:46.660
yet, in my lifetime, we have damaged it more severely than in the whole of the rest of

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:54.700
human history. Indeed, significant parts of it now are in danger of total destruction.

00:00:54.700 --> 00:01:02.100
When I first came to Borneo in 1956, the rainforest stretched unbroken on either side of the river

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:15.780
for hundreds of miles. Today, it's very different. Just beyond the trees lining the riverbank,

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:22.420
there is nothing but oil palm plantations. And the forest and all the rich variety of

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:32.860
animals and plants that it had once contained has been destroyed. And yet, as we have transformed

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:45.380
the natural world, so our attitudes towards it have changed fundamentally. Again and again,

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:50.740
I've seen the impoverishment and desolation caused by the way we have ruthlessly taken

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:58.020
what we want from the land, no matter what the cost. But I have also seen how the natural

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:07.460
world, given just the slightest chance, can manage to survive. And I have met the far-sighted

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:13.900
and dedicated conservationists who've labored to protect it, people who, by their own example,

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:23.940
have shown that there is something that can be done about it.

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:31.500
I was born in 1926, at the end of the age of the great naturalist collectors. It was

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:37.100
a time when it was perfectly acceptable to go out and collect creatures from the wild.

00:02:37.100 --> 00:02:42.820
If the London Zoo wanted a new animal or a replacement, it simply commissioned a collector

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:49.140
to go out and get it. And in the 1950s, as a young television producer obsessed with

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:54.380
the natural world, I was delighted when we got permission to go along with an expedition

00:02:54.380 --> 00:03:00.420
from the London Zoo. It was going to go to West Africa and be headed by one of the zoo's

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:06.180
animal collecting experts, Jack Lester. I thought it would be a good idea if we called

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:12.060
the series Quest for something or other. So I asked Jack Lester whether, in fact, there

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:16.500
was an animal there that we could have a quest for that no one had seen before. He

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:22.820
said, oh, yes. And it's called Piccathartes gymnocephalus. So I said, well, that's not

00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:28.300
really a very catchy quest for Piccathartes gymnocephalus. It's another name. And Jack

00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:35.020
said, yes, it's also called the bald-headed rockfowl. I said, well, even a quest for a

00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:39.980
bald-headed rockfowl doesn't likely to grab people, you know. So in the end, we just called

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:57.540
it Zoo Quest. We spent weeks traveling around the country collecting all kinds of mammals,

00:03:57.540 --> 00:04:05.300
reptiles, and birds. Everywhere we went, we showed people a picture of Piccathartes and

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:14.740
finally found a village chief who said the birds nested nearby. And so they did. In the

00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:20.100
finished programs, of course, we didn't reveal this immediately. Instead, we ended each by

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:28.540
saying, so we went on to look for Piccathartes. Nonetheless, we were a bit concerned as to

00:04:28.540 --> 00:04:34.660
whether anyone would really care about Piccathartes. But I was reassured when I was traveling down

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:41.540
Oxford Street in an open car and a bus driver leaned out of his cab and he said, hello,

00:04:41.540 --> 00:04:49.140
Dave. Well, are we or are we not going to find Piccabloody Farties? So I knew that actually

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:56.780
we had made an impact with somebody. And the bus driver got his answer in the last episode.

00:04:56.780 --> 00:05:01.660
We took our places behind the hide. And now came the most tense moment of the expedition,

00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:08.140
the moment for which we'd all waited so long. Would we see the adult birds? And then suddenly

00:05:08.140 --> 00:05:13.220
we saw one a few yards away in the twilight of the bush preening itself. This was enormous

00:05:13.220 --> 00:05:18.460
excitement. Then up it fluttered onto the nest. And as it did so, the other parent flew

00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:24.300
across and drove the first one away. This was a great thrill for us. For as this happened,

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:33.800
we became the first Europeans ever to see the white-necked Piccathartes on its nest.

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:39.340
Having filmed Piccathartes, we managed to collect a young nestling and brought it back

00:05:39.340 --> 00:05:45.980
together with sunbirds and emerald starlings to live here in the birdhouse in the London

00:05:45.980 --> 00:05:54.540
Zoo. It had been my first opportunity to film animals in the wild. And this happy collaboration

00:05:54.540 --> 00:06:02.780
with the London Zoo resulted in a whole succession of ZooQuest series. Sadly, after the first,

00:06:02.780 --> 00:06:07.380
Jack became seriously ill. So I took over and tried to give the impression that I knew

00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:14.620
what I was doing. It's important to grab his tail as soon as you grab his head. Otherwise,

00:06:14.620 --> 00:06:22.660
he'll wrap his great coils around you and give you a very nasty squeeze. I was more

00:06:22.660 --> 00:06:28.540
than happy that we'd been able to take it away without it harming us. First, I grabbed

00:06:28.540 --> 00:06:32.940
the tail with my left hand and then tickled his tummy with my right so that he doubled

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:38.100
up, lost his grip and out he came.

00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:46.500
Of course, I wouldn't behave like that today. Things have changed. Thanks to their breeding

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:52.360
programmes, zoos can get most of what they want without going to catch them in the wild.

00:06:52.360 --> 00:06:59.220
But that was then. Caring for the creatures we collected took so much time, it eventually

00:06:59.220 --> 00:07:08.180
became part of the programme's story. Once the animals we had collected had settled in

00:07:08.180 --> 00:07:13.260
at the zoo, we got permission to take some of the more interesting ones to the studios

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:17.940
to show them off on live television.

00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:23.300
And here he is, twice as large, I should say, but still just as hungry and still making

00:07:23.300 --> 00:07:28.580
this extraordinary little noise which he used to make out there in Borneo. And here he is

00:07:28.580 --> 00:07:36.940
in the studio. He can bite. He's got quite powerful fangs. I have been bitten by a python.

00:07:36.940 --> 00:07:46.260
It doesn't hurt much. Helping me control this python is Mr Landworn from the Reptile House

00:07:46.260 --> 00:07:52.860
in the London Zoo, who in fact has it in his care now. But he's quite a handful now, isn't

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:58.060
he? You can quite imagine how these powerful coils really give you quite a crunch.

00:07:58.060 --> 00:08:06.340
Our attitudes to wildlife were so very different in the 50s. But then they were about to undergo

00:08:06.340 --> 00:08:15.300
a transformation.

00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:19.220
Ducks and geese are decreasing in the world rather rapidly. It would be a great pity,

00:08:19.220 --> 00:08:24.940
I think, if they were allowed to disappear altogether or even to become extremely rare.

00:08:24.940 --> 00:08:29.820
In the marshy fields we've built special paddocks and in them we've established this

00:08:29.820 --> 00:08:33.460
collection of ducks and geese and swans.

00:08:33.460 --> 00:08:39.400
As a student, there was one person, perhaps more than anyone else, who fuelled my excitement

00:08:39.400 --> 00:08:45.780
about the natural world. He was the most celebrated broadcaster of his time. On radio, of course,

00:08:45.780 --> 00:08:51.980
there was no television. Little did I think that within a few years he and I were to become

00:08:51.980 --> 00:09:00.180
friends. That man was Peter Scott, who founded the Wildfowl and Wetlands Trust and created

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:06.300
its first reserve around his home here at Slimbridge. Peter Scott made me realise for

00:09:06.300 --> 00:09:11.600
the first time that there were species of animal around the world that were in danger

00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:16.140
of becoming extinct. It was a radical idea at the time.

00:09:16.140 --> 00:09:21.900
Well, if we decide that we have got a responsibility to prevent animals from becoming extinct,

00:09:21.900 --> 00:09:27.860
what can we do about it? Well, in extreme cases, we can, and I think we should, take

00:09:27.860 --> 00:09:34.180
into captivity a proportion of the population into some zoo or park or reserve and try and

00:09:34.180 --> 00:09:40.460
breed them there and build up the stock. Now, here at the Wildfowl Trust we have done that

00:09:40.460 --> 00:09:47.740
with the nene, or Hawaiian goose.

00:09:47.740 --> 00:09:53.340
The nene evolved on the island of Hawaii, but in the 19th century colonial settlers

00:09:53.340 --> 00:09:59.620
brought dogs, pigs, rats and mongooses, all of which preyed heavily on the nene. By the

00:09:59.620 --> 00:10:07.420
late 1940s there were only 30 individual birds left.

00:10:07.420 --> 00:10:14.940
Peter Scott, as a young man, had been a passionate hunter of wildfowl. Now he became their saviour.

00:10:14.940 --> 00:10:20.020
In 1950 he arranged for a few of them to be brought halfway around the world to Slimbridge

00:10:20.020 --> 00:10:23.780
so that he could try to breed them in captivity.

00:10:23.780 --> 00:10:30.460
And he succeeded, because these are some of their descendants.

00:10:30.460 --> 00:10:37.860
Wonderfully tame. And now they have been introduced not only to other wildlife sanctuaries, but

00:10:37.860 --> 00:10:44.420
back to Hawaii.

00:10:44.420 --> 00:10:51.540
While I met Peter here at Slimbridge and seen these nene, it had never occurred to me that

00:10:51.540 --> 00:10:59.660
a species could become totally extinct in my lifetime. But Peter and the nene changed

00:10:59.660 --> 00:11:07.540
all that and I began to wonder seriously about what I myself could do to become involved

00:11:07.540 --> 00:11:12.660
in the protection of wildlife.

00:11:12.660 --> 00:11:17.540
In those days I was rather more interested in mammals than I was in birds, but nonetheless

00:11:17.540 --> 00:11:22.820
Peter and I regularly compared notes. One day I ran into him in the Natural History

00:11:22.820 --> 00:11:23.820
Museum.

00:11:23.820 --> 00:11:28.900
Where are you off to next? He said. I said we're going to Madagascar. Madagascar, he

00:11:28.900 --> 00:11:34.020
said. The Madagascar Potschard is one of the rarest ducks in the world, the only one that

00:11:34.020 --> 00:11:39.940
we haven't got in the collection at Slimbridge. And I said Peter, if you want Madagascar Potschard,

00:11:39.940 --> 00:11:45.300
leave it to me, I'll bring you back a pair. And off we went to Madagascar. Well of course

00:11:45.300 --> 00:11:52.220
actually I was in Madagascar looking for lemurs. And we got the first film ever of the Indri,

00:11:52.220 --> 00:11:56.380
with the biggest of the living lemurs and other things too. And the series was going

00:11:56.380 --> 00:12:02.260
down quite well when I happened to meet Peter again. And as I met him I suddenly thought,

00:12:02.260 --> 00:12:07.740
I forgot all about the Madagascar Potschard. So I went over and I said Peter I'm terribly

00:12:07.740 --> 00:12:13.500
sorry, we did look very hard but we never found your Madagascar Potschard. Didn't you?

00:12:13.500 --> 00:12:17.820
He said, oh I was looking at the show last night and there were about a thousand of them

00:12:17.820 --> 00:12:24.140
behind you as you were talking to camera. Clearly both my memory and my ornithology

00:12:24.140 --> 00:12:31.020
needed a bit of improvement. By now Peter had his own Natural History series on television.

00:12:31.020 --> 00:12:38.820
Which was called Look. At the same time he and others were devising a strategy for protecting

00:12:38.820 --> 00:12:46.620
wildlife worldwide. A World Wildlife Charter to meet what amounts to a state of emergency

00:12:46.620 --> 00:12:55.100
for wildlife. And now we've got a World Wildlife Fund which is being launched to give it teeth.

00:12:55.100 --> 00:13:03.380
In 1961 Peter became one of the founder members of the World Wildlife Fund as it then was.

00:13:03.380 --> 00:13:09.380
One of the most charismatic and endangered animals of the time was a Chinese creature,

00:13:09.380 --> 00:13:16.620
the Giant Panda. Its simple black and white form made it an excellent subject for a logo

00:13:16.620 --> 00:13:25.220
and Peter designed it. This is his original and to my mind much the best version. The

00:13:25.220 --> 00:13:31.260
Fund was the first international body to spend money on conservation projects around the

00:13:31.260 --> 00:13:37.740
world and one of its first projects was to help the endangered and rare animals on the

00:13:37.740 --> 00:13:58.700
Galapagos Islands. And these extraordinary islands still remain wonderlands today. This

00:13:58.700 --> 00:14:06.240
is a giant Galapagos tortoise. They live longer than any other animal on earth, well over

00:14:06.240 --> 00:14:16.420
150 years. They weigh up to a quarter of a ton and have shells over a metre across.

00:14:16.420 --> 00:14:25.700
They really are giants. Some 15 subspecies of these reptiles evolved on the Galapagos

00:14:25.700 --> 00:14:38.540
but in the 17th century human beings discovered the islands. The tortoises were a valuable

00:14:38.540 --> 00:14:45.100
source of fresh meat and visiting sailors took them away by the thousand. By the middle

00:14:45.100 --> 00:14:51.200
of the 20th century one third of the original subspecies had been totally exterminated and

00:14:51.200 --> 00:14:58.700
only 3,000 of the remainder still survived. In the early 1960s the World Wildlife Fund

00:14:58.700 --> 00:15:04.820
got involved with trying to halt the decline. They put money into the Charles Darwin Research

00:15:04.820 --> 00:15:10.020
Centre on the Galapagos which collected tortoise eggs from the wild and carefully raised them

00:15:10.020 --> 00:15:24.080
away from predators. By the 1970s when I first visited the Galapagos the first captive bred

00:15:24.080 --> 00:15:33.720
tortoises were ready to be released. And a dramatic discovery had been made on Pinter

00:15:33.720 --> 00:15:40.260
Island. The subspecies that evolved there had long been thought extinct but in 1971

00:15:40.260 --> 00:15:48.340
a single male tortoise was discovered there. He was brought back to the Charles Darwin

00:15:48.340 --> 00:15:55.900
Research Station where he quickly became a celebrity in his own right. This is the rarest

00:15:55.900 --> 00:16:07.960
living animal in all the world. There is none rarer. This is Lonesome George. It was hoped

00:16:07.960 --> 00:16:13.840
that a female Pinter tortoise might be found with which he could breed but it was not to

00:16:13.840 --> 00:16:25.380
be. Lonesome George it seems is doomed to be the last of his kind. Sadly he died in

00:16:25.380 --> 00:16:36.360
June 2012. But other surviving Galapagos tortoises have had to deal with a different threat.

00:16:36.360 --> 00:16:42.720
Goats. They were brought to the island long ago by both sailors and settlers and have

00:16:42.720 --> 00:16:50.880
now gone wild. They crop the vegetation so severely that there is little or nothing left

00:16:50.880 --> 00:16:57.680
for the tortoises. So the island's conservation authorities decided to eradicate feral goats

00:16:57.680 --> 00:17:02.520
on several of the islands so that the vegetation could recover and the tortoises get their

00:17:02.520 --> 00:17:10.840
natural food back. Now on Isabella Island as I saw for myself in 2008 the plants have

00:17:10.840 --> 00:17:22.040
returned to their former lushness and the tortoises future has been secured. Saving

00:17:22.040 --> 00:17:28.920
large dramatic species was one of conservationists first names but soon we realised that true

00:17:28.920 --> 00:17:35.400
conservation means protecting the entire habitat of which the spectacular species is just one

00:17:35.400 --> 00:17:45.720
element. And one way of doing that is to establish nature reserves or national parks. The first

00:17:45.720 --> 00:17:51.560
national park in Africa was created in 1925 around the volcanoes that lie in the heart

00:17:51.560 --> 00:17:57.920
of the continent. Its aim was to protect the rare mountain gorillas which were being killed

00:17:57.920 --> 00:18:04.040
by trophy hunters and poachers. What has happened there since has made it quite clear that it

00:18:04.040 --> 00:18:10.960
is not just the progression of governments drawing lines on a map. Very often it requires

00:18:10.960 --> 00:18:20.160
the passion and determination of one highly motivated individual as I saw myself in Rwanda.

00:18:20.160 --> 00:18:25.280
An American woman Diane Fosse had been studying the mountain gorillas in the Virunga volcanoes

00:18:25.280 --> 00:18:32.880
national park since 1967. By patiently sitting near to them year after year she had eventually

00:18:32.880 --> 00:18:41.900
won their complete trust to a quite astonishing degree. In 1978 she agreed that we might come

00:18:41.900 --> 00:18:55.000
with cameras to film them. She introduced us to the gorillas in the sense that they

00:18:55.000 --> 00:19:00.680
saw that we were Diane so I suspect that that may well have been that they therefore thought

00:19:00.680 --> 00:19:28.280
we were okay. But without Diane that sequence could never have happened.

00:19:28.280 --> 00:19:38.360
There is more meaning and mutual understanding in exchanging of plants with a gorilla than

00:19:38.360 --> 00:19:47.520
any other animal I know. They're so similar. Their sight, their hearing, their sense of

00:19:47.520 --> 00:19:57.120
smell are so similar to ours that we see the world in the same way as they do. They live

00:19:57.120 --> 00:20:04.920
in the same sort of social groups, hardly permanent family relationships. They walk

00:20:04.920 --> 00:20:12.920
around on the ground as we do though they're immensely more powerful than we are. And so

00:20:12.920 --> 00:20:21.000
if ever there was a possibility of escaping the human condition and living imaginatively.

00:20:21.000 --> 00:20:36.000
In another creatures world it must be with a gorilla. And this is how they spend most

00:20:36.000 --> 00:20:44.840
of their time lounging on the ground grooming one another. Sometimes they even allow others

00:20:44.840 --> 00:21:00.240
to join in.

00:21:00.240 --> 00:21:05.640
What that sequence didn't show but which the still pictures I took at the time did was

00:21:05.640 --> 00:21:11.160
the way the gorillas were fascinated by our equipment. One of them was very interested

00:21:11.160 --> 00:21:16.200
in the long sort of sausage shaped housing that holds the microphone and you can see

00:21:16.200 --> 00:21:21.960
this young male just feeding it, seeing what it is. And also they were fascinated by the

00:21:21.960 --> 00:21:27.400
camera and they came to Martin Saunders who was the cameraman and they were peering inside

00:21:27.400 --> 00:21:35.080
the camera to see that they could see another animal inside it. And finally the adult male,

00:21:35.080 --> 00:21:41.320
the big silverback appeared. Diane's name for him was Beethoven and Beethoven was a

00:21:41.320 --> 00:21:46.880
huge powerful animal and really quite alarming because if he lost his temper with you he

00:21:46.880 --> 00:21:51.920
could simply smash your skull with one blow of his fist. The thing you don't do is pick

00:21:51.920 --> 00:21:57.120
up your camera and look directly at him. That's a challenging thing to do. So I have quite

00:21:57.120 --> 00:22:02.720
a lot of pictures of Beethoven gazing to the right or to the left or even looking away

00:22:02.720 --> 00:22:16.680
from me. Yeah, so he is. But behind this extraordinary encounter lay a tragic and shocking reality.

00:22:16.680 --> 00:22:25.080
We had arrived in Diane Fosse's camp in January 1978, just days after Diane's favourite gorilla,

00:22:25.080 --> 00:22:31.880
a young male, his name was Digit, had been savagely and brutally killed by poachers.

00:22:31.880 --> 00:22:36.360
Diane was grief stricken, it was though she had lost a child and on top of that she was

00:22:36.360 --> 00:22:46.560
in extremely poor health, spitting blood. We became witness to a slow motion tragedy.

00:22:46.560 --> 00:22:50.880
Gorillas had been illegally killed in the Vorunga Volcanoes National Park throughout

00:22:50.880 --> 00:22:58.360
the 60s and 70s. When Diane had arrived there were about 500 left but there were only about

00:22:58.360 --> 00:23:04.600
half that number at the time of our visit and Diane had taken it upon herself to organise

00:23:04.600 --> 00:23:11.800
anti-poaching patrols. Never before had it been so clear to me that a species was heading

00:23:11.800 --> 00:23:18.080
for disaster. It was just Diane Fosse who was standing between the mountain gorillas

00:23:18.080 --> 00:23:25.160
and extinction. On our last evening at her camp Diane called me to her sick bed and made

00:23:25.160 --> 00:23:30.600
me promise to do something to help save the gorillas. And when I got back to Britain I

00:23:30.600 --> 00:23:37.040
kept that promise and got together with other conservationists and jointly we created the

00:23:37.040 --> 00:23:46.880
mountain gorilla fund. The sequence with the gorillas caused something of a sensation and

00:23:46.880 --> 00:23:51.600
helped people realise that these relatives of ours were not only endangered but had to

00:23:51.600 --> 00:24:00.120
be helped. Once Diane's health had improved she resumed her efforts to protect the gorillas

00:24:00.120 --> 00:24:08.560
and their habitat. She fought as hard as she could to prevent great areas of the forest

00:24:08.560 --> 00:24:16.320
from being cut down and turned into farmland. And she continued her battles with the poachers,

00:24:16.320 --> 00:24:23.000
destroying their snares and arresting them when her patrols captured them red-handed.

00:24:23.000 --> 00:24:28.360
Although there's no doubt that Diane Fosse's anti-poaching methods were controversial and

00:24:28.360 --> 00:24:33.920
certainly antagonised many of the local people, nonetheless it succeeded in saving much of

00:24:33.920 --> 00:24:41.120
the forest. And today in spite of the dreadful civil wars that have since devastated Rwanda,

00:24:41.120 --> 00:24:48.320
there are twice as many gorillas as there were when we were there. But they're still

00:24:48.320 --> 00:24:54.280
threatened because of the great speed at which the human population of the region is increasing

00:24:54.280 --> 00:25:03.180
and that danger is in fact a global one. You and I belong to the most widespread and dominant

00:25:03.180 --> 00:25:09.420
species of animal on earth. We live on the ice caps of the pole and in the tropical jungles

00:25:09.420 --> 00:25:14.360
of the equator. We've climbed the highest mountain and dived deep into the seas. We've

00:25:14.360 --> 00:25:21.480
even left the earth and set foot on the moon. And we're certainly the most numerous large

00:25:21.480 --> 00:25:27.760
animal. There are something like 4,000 million of us today and we've reached this position

00:25:27.760 --> 00:25:35.000
with meteoric speed. It's all happened within the last 2,000 years or so. We seem to have

00:25:35.000 --> 00:25:42.800
broken loose from the restrictions that have governed the activities and numbers of other

00:25:42.800 --> 00:25:50.640
animals. That was St Peter's Square in Rome in 1978. I said then that there were 4,000

00:25:50.640 --> 00:26:00.080
million, that is 4 billion of us on this planet, twice as many as when I was born. Today that

00:26:00.080 --> 00:26:06.720
has nearly doubled yet again. There are now over 7 billion of us and by some estimates

00:26:06.720 --> 00:26:23.000
there may be 9 billion in 2050. That growth is largely attributable to medical advances

00:26:23.000 --> 00:26:32.480
and to the highly efficient ways we've found to grow our food. In just a few thousand years

00:26:32.480 --> 00:26:38.720
the revolution of agriculture has spread to virtually all human societies. Today over

00:26:38.720 --> 00:26:45.720
a third of the surface of the land is devoted to producing food for human beings. And that

00:26:45.720 --> 00:27:03.040
has changed some landscapes in the most dramatic way. Our scientific and technological ingenuity

00:27:03.040 --> 00:27:09.800
has enormously increased agricultural productivity in the last 60 years. World grain production

00:27:09.800 --> 00:27:15.080
has more than tripled. But even that has not been able to keep pace with the needs of the

00:27:15.080 --> 00:27:23.200
world's growing human population. In some parts of the world the natural forest was

00:27:23.200 --> 00:27:31.100
cleared for agriculture many centuries ago. But elsewhere that transformation has happened

00:27:31.100 --> 00:27:46.120
in my lifetime. When I first came to Borneo in 1956 all this was rainforest. Now all those

00:27:46.120 --> 00:27:52.560
trees have gone. The logging industry took out the wood. The palm oil industry cleared

00:27:52.560 --> 00:28:02.340
what remained of the forest and replaced it with its own uniformed plantations. All those

00:28:02.340 --> 00:28:08.400
extra human mouths have to be fed and the country needs the cash. But the effect on

00:28:08.400 --> 00:28:18.440
the natural world has been catastrophic. Few have suffered more than the orangutans. Many

00:28:18.440 --> 00:28:24.220
adults were killed as the forest was cleared. If their babies didn't die with them then

00:28:24.220 --> 00:28:31.380
they were usually taken and sold as pets. A few fortunate ones ended up in sanctuaries

00:28:31.380 --> 00:28:40.040
like this one at Sepilok. These baby orangs are orphans mostly rescued from the pet trade.

00:28:40.040 --> 00:28:46.720
It's easy to see why they make such engaging pets when they're young. Indeed when I was

00:28:46.720 --> 00:28:54.840
here 50 years ago I had one as a pet which I became very fond of. His mother had been

00:28:54.840 --> 00:29:02.780
killed by a villager as she raided his banana plantation. London Zoo I knew wanted to establish

00:29:02.780 --> 00:29:09.000
an orang breeding colony so he joined our floating menagerie. But it wasn't long before

00:29:09.000 --> 00:29:20.620
Charlie as we'd cushioned him began to calm down. Slowly we managed to win his confidence.

00:29:20.620 --> 00:29:25.900
And then for the first time four days after we'd had him we encouraged him to come right

00:29:25.900 --> 00:29:40.980
outside his cage. And here is Charlie safe and sound back in London. Hey Charlie Charlie.

00:29:40.980 --> 00:29:48.040
Oh dear. That's it. And with him is Mr Smith the head keeper of the monkey house and how

00:29:48.040 --> 00:29:53.440
is he Mr Smith? Very much recovered from his long and arduous journey. Yes. And he's going

00:29:53.440 --> 00:29:57.860
to settle down and I think he's going to be with us for a very long time. Good. And that

00:29:57.860 --> 00:30:03.440
he was. And a few years after his arrival at the zoo he took a shine to a young female

00:30:03.440 --> 00:30:10.760
who was already there. Back in 1961 I went into the ape house in London Zoo to see Charlie

00:30:10.760 --> 00:30:16.240
as I often did and the head keeper came over and he said I've got good news he said. You're

00:30:16.240 --> 00:30:21.940
about to become a grandfather. Really I said. Yes he said. Your young Charlie has fathered

00:30:21.940 --> 00:30:27.880
a baby and should be born in a few months time. And as grandfather he said you have

00:30:27.880 --> 00:30:34.200
the privilege of christening it. So eventually I decided to be called Bulu which in Malay

00:30:34.200 --> 00:30:46.760
means little hairy one. Bulu? Shall we have Bulu? Now this is Charlie's daughter. All

00:30:46.760 --> 00:30:53.080
right. All right. All right. Bulu was the first orangutan born in Britain and she was

00:30:53.080 --> 00:31:01.520
just as endearing as Charlie had been. I look back on those days when I had Charlie the

00:31:01.520 --> 00:31:07.860
baby orang with mixed feelings because the fact of the matter is that these are not pets.

00:31:07.860 --> 00:31:14.240
These are wild animals and they should be in the wild. The problem is that although

00:31:14.240 --> 00:31:20.800
many people in Borneo support the rehabilitation of orangutan their rainforest home continues

00:31:20.800 --> 00:31:28.960
to be destroyed as the rest of the world increases its demand for palm oil. So the question that

00:31:28.960 --> 00:31:34.140
hangs over these orang's future is whether there will be enough forest left for them

00:31:34.140 --> 00:31:41.280
to return to when they've grown up. Strong measures will have to be taken if that is

00:31:41.280 --> 00:31:59.760
to be so. There is one place where our destructive impact on the planet is less immediately obvious.

00:31:59.760 --> 00:32:12.640
The oceans. I can see its tail just under my boat here and it's coming up. It's coming

00:32:12.640 --> 00:32:22.400
up. There. The blue whale is 100 feet long, 30 meters. Nothing like that can grow on them

00:32:22.400 --> 00:32:29.640
because no bone is strong enough to support such a bulk. Only in the sea can you get such

00:32:29.640 --> 00:32:46.800
a huge size as that magnificent creature. I had to wait until I was 76 years old to see

00:32:46.800 --> 00:32:53.200
my first blue whale. Part of what made the encounter so special was that for much of

00:32:53.200 --> 00:32:58.560
my lifetime blue whales were being killed at such a rate that it seemed quite possible

00:32:58.560 --> 00:33:05.320
that they would become extinct before I ever saw one. The fact that they've survived is

00:33:05.320 --> 00:33:11.560
a conservation triumph. And that only happened because there was a fundamental change worldwide

00:33:11.560 --> 00:33:22.680
in people's attitudes to whales. Men had hunted whales for centuries, primarily for

00:33:22.680 --> 00:33:28.160
the sake of the oil in their blubber. And the skeletons of just a few of them ended

00:33:28.160 --> 00:33:36.560
up here in the Natural History Museum. When I was growing up, whale products were used

00:33:36.560 --> 00:33:42.560
mostly in food. I must have unconsciously eaten a fair amount of blubber because it

00:33:42.560 --> 00:33:48.760
was an ingredient of margarine. And during the war, when meat was really scarce, I certainly

00:33:48.760 --> 00:33:55.800
ate what was euphemistically called Arctic steak whale meat. But it never occurred to

00:33:55.800 --> 00:34:03.520
me that whales could actually be endangered. But improved methods of tracking and killing

00:34:03.520 --> 00:34:16.640
whales was reducing their numbers alarmingly. By the 1960s, there were fewer than 2,000

00:34:16.640 --> 00:34:24.200
blue whales surviving, just 1% of their probable original population. The species seemed headed

00:34:24.200 --> 00:34:33.560
towards extinction until whaling nations finally banned the hunting of blue whales. What changed

00:34:33.560 --> 00:34:38.720
the fortunes of the other great whales were anti-whaling campaigners who turned whole

00:34:38.720 --> 00:34:46.160
nations against the industry. And once again, Peter Scott helped show the way. It was Peter

00:34:46.160 --> 00:34:53.520
Scott who first made me and many, many others aware of the plight of the great whales. By

00:34:53.520 --> 00:34:59.920
the 1970s, he and other activists like Greenpeace were at the forefront of the campaigns to

00:34:59.920 --> 00:35:11.080
prevent their slaughter. It was an issue that I could not avoid. This beautiful, intelligent,

00:35:11.080 --> 00:35:17.000
astounding creature is a killer whale. There are about 80 different kinds of whales in

00:35:17.000 --> 00:35:24.240
the world. Whales, of course, are warm-blooded like ourselves and, as we are belatedly beginning

00:35:24.240 --> 00:35:29.960
to discover, extremely intelligent. Surely they are among the most fascinating creatures

00:35:29.960 --> 00:35:37.920
in the world. The film that follows is made by a group of people who passionately believe

00:35:37.920 --> 00:35:47.960
that the whales should be protected. They call themselves Greenpeace.

00:35:47.960 --> 00:36:08.480
Hello Vostok. We are Canadian. Hello Vostok. We are Canadian. We are asking you, from your

00:36:08.480 --> 00:36:18.760
position of strength and power, to grant us a small request. Please stop killing the whales.

00:36:18.760 --> 00:36:25.040
We are men and women and we speak for children and we are all saying, please stop killing

00:36:25.040 --> 00:36:33.520
the whales. It would take nearly another decade of activism by Greenpeace and patient negotiation

00:36:33.520 --> 00:36:40.920
by Peter Scott and others before a total ban on commercial whaling came into force. Since

00:36:40.920 --> 00:36:47.760
1986, whales have only been legally killed by indigenous communities or for scientific

00:36:47.760 --> 00:36:54.400
purposes. I remember very vividly Peter saying to me

00:36:54.400 --> 00:37:02.320
once, I will die a happy man if I can think that we have saved the great whales. Well,

00:37:02.320 --> 00:37:11.680
as far as the blue whale is concerned, we have gone a long way to achieving that ambition.

00:37:11.680 --> 00:37:17.840
Today the world's blue whale population appears to be recovering slowly. It has doubled in

00:37:17.840 --> 00:37:32.160
the last 50 years to perhaps as many as 4,500. Of course, it's not just the big charismatic

00:37:32.160 --> 00:37:38.040
species that we are exterminating. Life on earth is a complex web and we ignore the millions

00:37:38.040 --> 00:37:43.640
of tiny creatures in it at our peril. One kind of animal is right now in the grip of

00:37:43.640 --> 00:37:51.040
the greatest extinction event since the disappearance of the dinosaurs. Animals like this, amphibians.

00:37:51.040 --> 00:37:57.920
Globally the numbers of amphibians are declining at an alarming rate. One third of all species

00:37:57.920 --> 00:38:04.880
are now critically endangered. In the rainforest of Costa Rica in the late

00:38:04.880 --> 00:38:14.200
70s, we filmed the Monteverde toad. Ten years later, inexplicably, it had become extinct.

00:38:14.200 --> 00:38:19.600
It was only in the last few years that the mystery of what killed the toad was finally

00:38:19.600 --> 00:38:26.480
solved and that was not before many other species of amphibians had also died out. In

00:38:26.480 --> 00:38:33.040
fact, while we were filming Life in Cold Blood in 2007, I actually witnessed the extinction

00:38:33.040 --> 00:38:42.360
in the wild of the Palamanian golden frog which fell victim to the same insidious killer.

00:38:42.360 --> 00:38:47.320
Individual males set up their territories beside the river and then wait for females

00:38:47.320 --> 00:38:53.400
to turn up. And since good positions for the territory are not common, they may have to

00:38:53.400 --> 00:39:02.760
hold them against intruders. And here one comes. Just in case his call

00:39:02.760 --> 00:39:18.760
is inaudible, he makes his message clear with a wave. And his rival waves back. He repeats

00:39:18.760 --> 00:39:28.320
his message so there's no misunderstanding. Sadly, there are no longer any Palamanian

00:39:28.320 --> 00:39:35.320
golden frogs waving in the wild and the disease that killed them is now sweeping around the

00:39:35.320 --> 00:39:41.780
world, exterminating hundreds of different species of amphibians.

00:39:41.780 --> 00:39:48.480
The killer is a fungus. It's highly infectious and believed to have originated in South Africa

00:39:48.480 --> 00:39:55.160
from where it was transported by the international trade in captive animals. It was spreading

00:39:55.160 --> 00:39:59.800
across Panama while we were filming and when we had finished, scientists collected the

00:39:59.800 --> 00:40:05.360
last few survivors and took them into a specially quarantined building where other endangered

00:40:05.360 --> 00:40:12.340
amphibians were being kept. Here they may breed and then if a cure for the fungus is

00:40:12.340 --> 00:40:20.800
found or it runs its course in the wild, the frogs may be returned to their former home.

00:40:20.800 --> 00:40:32.080
In the last 60 years, I've come face to face with many species that we've put at risk.

00:40:32.080 --> 00:40:50.840
Sea otters, chimpanzee, manatee. Sadly, this magnificent animal is getting rarer and rarer.

00:40:50.840 --> 00:40:59.880
How many of these wonderful things will still be around in another 60 years? What an extraordinary

00:40:59.880 --> 00:41:05.600
world. Although the threat to the natural world from humanity has never been greater

00:41:05.600 --> 00:41:12.720
than it is today, there are nonetheless causes for hope here and there. In recent decades,

00:41:12.720 --> 00:41:17.960
when people have become involved with the local population of animals, they have started

00:41:17.960 --> 00:41:24.000
to take part in the conservation process and that's certainly the case here in Borneo

00:41:24.000 --> 00:41:31.480
and the caves at Comanton. The only visitors here when we first came in 1972 were the local

00:41:31.480 --> 00:41:44.360
people and the people came to the cave for one particular and extraordinary purpose.

00:41:44.360 --> 00:41:50.960
They collect what is surely one of the strangest commodities to be found in any cuisine. It's

00:41:50.960 --> 00:42:00.080
so valuable that they risk their lives to get it. They're harvesting the nests that

00:42:00.080 --> 00:42:11.080
swiftlets construct using their own glutinous spittle. And this is the end product of all

00:42:11.080 --> 00:42:18.080
this labour and sweat and danger and sheer courage. One can't help wondering who it was

00:42:18.080 --> 00:42:22.560
who first looked at these extraordinary objects and said that it would be great for making

00:42:22.560 --> 00:42:28.920
soup out of. But whoever he was, he lived over a thousand years ago because there are

00:42:28.920 --> 00:42:34.520
Chinese records of the ninth and tenth centuries which speak of the wonderful delicacies, bird's

00:42:34.520 --> 00:42:44.320
nests, that you can get from Borneo. I wanted to see what all the fuss was about so I went

00:42:44.320 --> 00:42:51.800
into a local restaurant in Sandakan to see what bird's nest soup actually tastes like.

00:42:51.800 --> 00:42:58.440
The consistency perhaps is a little odd, it's a little sort of gelatinous, but for the rest

00:42:58.440 --> 00:43:06.200
of it, well, I'm afraid there is one great secret about bird's nests. The fact of the

00:43:06.200 --> 00:43:14.400
matter is that pure bird's nests taste of nothing whatsoever, provided that it's been

00:43:14.400 --> 00:43:22.560
well cleaned. Even in the 70s, the bird's nests were so valuable that there was an obvious

00:43:22.560 --> 00:43:28.760
risk that the cave would be over-exploited. But today, that risk is even greater. A nest

00:43:28.760 --> 00:43:35.520
like this is worth as much as £100. If you take too many of them, then the birds will

00:43:35.520 --> 00:43:41.960
have nowhere to raise their young and the colony is doomed. But a total ban would deprive

00:43:41.960 --> 00:43:52.280
the local people of a very important part of their income. So a plan was agreed. Some

00:43:52.280 --> 00:43:59.680
caves should be regularly harvested, others should be protected from any human interference

00:43:59.680 --> 00:44:08.200
and one should be open for the public to visit and wonder. It's an almost ideal situation.

00:44:08.200 --> 00:44:14.200
The local economy benefits, the wildlife benefits and an ancient tradition with luck is kept

00:44:14.200 --> 00:44:27.720
alive for many years to come. Other creatures in Borneo are now also being protected by

00:44:27.720 --> 00:44:37.120
people who once put them in danger. This is Selangun Island off the northern coast of

00:44:37.120 --> 00:44:45.600
Borneo and turtles come up here onto beaches like this at night in order to lay their eggs.

00:44:45.600 --> 00:44:51.760
Back in the 1950s, local people would come to such places in order to dig up those eggs

00:44:51.760 --> 00:45:02.080
and eat them and I have to admit they weren't the only people to do that. If turtles used

00:45:02.080 --> 00:45:06.080
this beach, it occurred to me that there might be a chance that we could find a turtle's

00:45:06.080 --> 00:45:12.680
nest with eggs, which would be a very welcome addition to the rice, bananas and bully beef

00:45:12.680 --> 00:45:23.760
on which we had been living almost entirely for the past week. And here, buried three

00:45:23.760 --> 00:45:32.280
feet deep were the eggs. There were 88 eggs in that nest, enough to provide us with breakfast

00:45:32.280 --> 00:45:39.600
for many days to come and they were all produced by one female turtle. Looking back, it all

00:45:39.600 --> 00:45:45.280
seems rather shocking and I hadn't got a clue how to cook them. We had cheerfully added

00:45:45.280 --> 00:45:53.640
as much salt as if we were dealing with chickens eggs. The result, there were no doubt that

00:45:53.640 --> 00:46:02.200
in nourishing wasn't, I'm afraid, particularly delicious. Turtle eggs may not have been to

00:46:02.200 --> 00:46:07.040
my taste, but the local people loved them and they were an important source not only

00:46:07.040 --> 00:46:13.480
of nutriment but income. The trouble was that the human population was growing so fast that

00:46:13.480 --> 00:46:25.320
the turtle eggs were being collected in huge numbers and turtles worldwide were in decline.

00:46:25.320 --> 00:46:34.760
In the decades that followed, the Malaysian government stepped in to save their turtles.

00:46:34.760 --> 00:46:40.480
During the eggs was banned, a hatchery established on Selangor Island, which people visit to

00:46:40.480 --> 00:46:49.480
see what's going on. During the breeding season, the eggs are collected from the beach

00:46:49.480 --> 00:46:54.920
and re-buried in the hatchery, each clutch being kept together inside its own little

00:46:54.920 --> 00:47:05.240
fence. But it's only after dark that the adult turtles reveal themselves, crawling

00:47:05.240 --> 00:47:18.020
out of the sea and laying their eggs to the delight of the onlookers. The visitors pay

00:47:18.020 --> 00:47:22.480
good money for the privilege of watching the turtles at close quarters and that gives an

00:47:22.480 --> 00:47:31.480
income to the local people. Once the eggs hatch, the youngsters are collected and taken

00:47:31.480 --> 00:47:49.080
down to the shore. Off you go. Off you go. Millions of baby turtles have now been released

00:47:49.080 --> 00:47:54.320
under this conservation programme and as a consequence, the population of adult green

00:47:54.320 --> 00:48:03.840
turtles here is now increasing. But the survival of green turtles needs more than their protection

00:48:03.840 --> 00:48:15.040
by local people at their nesting beaches. Turtles migrate. They swim across national

00:48:15.040 --> 00:48:27.880
borders into unprotected foreign waters and that can be a problem. It's now clear that

00:48:27.880 --> 00:48:34.080
many conservation projects will only succeed in the long term if they transcend national

00:48:34.080 --> 00:48:41.200
boundaries and allow wildlife to cross frontiers without hindrance and that's exactly what's

00:48:41.200 --> 00:48:48.520
happening here in the rainforest in the island of Borneo. Indonesia, Malaysia and Brunei

00:48:48.520 --> 00:48:56.360
signed the Heart of Borneo Agreement in 2007, declaring that the rainforest will be protected

00:48:56.360 --> 00:49:02.420
while allowing sustainable use and access by local people. This sort of international

00:49:02.420 --> 00:49:08.760
cross-border cooperation is vital if we're to safeguard an area's wildlife and ultimately

00:49:08.760 --> 00:49:16.040
the health of the planet. And thinking about the health of the planet as a whole was not

00:49:16.040 --> 00:49:23.240
something many people did until one truly extraordinary and historic event. The engines

00:49:23.240 --> 00:49:34.880
are on. Four, three, two, one, zero. We have commit. We have lift off. Lift off at 7.51

00:49:34.880 --> 00:49:44.400
am. Pictures of the launch of Apollo 8 arrived in Britain back in 1968 by way of the BBC's

00:49:44.400 --> 00:49:50.320
central control room here in the Television Centre in London where I had a job as a network

00:49:50.320 --> 00:49:57.320
controller. Looking at the top is the North Pole. In the centre, just north of the centre,

00:49:57.320 --> 00:50:04.440
is South America. All the way down to Cape Horn. Those images were instrumental in changing

00:50:04.440 --> 00:50:13.520
the way that many of us viewed the planet. We began to think globally. Looking at the

00:50:13.520 --> 00:50:20.720
Earth from outer space made us realise just how small our world is and how finite its

00:50:20.720 --> 00:50:30.000
resources. It also helped us understand that we have to cherish not just individual species

00:50:30.000 --> 00:50:36.580
nor even individual patches of wilderness, but the whole planet as a single integrated

00:50:36.580 --> 00:50:47.440
ecosystem. But back in 1968, few people could imagine that the activities of just one species,

00:50:47.440 --> 00:50:52.640
our own, could interfere with the way that the planet worked, that we could actually

00:50:52.640 --> 00:51:08.760
change the climate of the Earth. It was in the oceans that this threat first became apparent.

00:51:08.760 --> 00:51:14.800
I'll never forget the first time I put my head beneath the surface of the sea and saw

00:51:14.800 --> 00:51:22.360
all around me a coral reef in all its complexity and richness and almost unbelievable beauty.

00:51:22.360 --> 00:51:38.800
I've been enthralled by coral reefs ever since. If the jungle is the place on land where there

00:51:38.800 --> 00:51:49.880
are the greatest number and the greatest variety of life, then this, the coral reef, is surely

00:51:49.880 --> 00:51:59.720
the jungle of the sea. Although coral reefs occupy just 1% of the oceans, they support

00:51:59.720 --> 00:52:09.400
a quarter of all their fish. The fragility of these complex ecosystems suddenly became

00:52:09.400 --> 00:52:20.040
alarmingly clear in 1998. Almost overnight, in oceans all around the globe, coral turned

00:52:20.040 --> 00:52:26.760
white. The temperature of the sea had risen and it had devastated 16% of the world's coral

00:52:26.760 --> 00:52:33.520
reefs. Even the rise of a single degree centigrade can be enough to kill the organisms that build

00:52:33.520 --> 00:52:40.960
the coral, leaving their limestone skeletons a naked white. If the rise is brief, then

00:52:40.960 --> 00:52:47.200
the coral can recover, but if it is sustained, then the coral may die completely and this

00:52:47.200 --> 00:52:54.920
coral bleaching hints at an even bigger problem. The average temperature of our planet has

00:52:54.920 --> 00:53:01.040
increased by 0.7 degrees centigrade over the last century and it seems likely to rise still

00:53:01.040 --> 00:53:10.960
further and that could lead to changes in sea level. Even a very small rise in sea temperature

00:53:10.960 --> 00:53:17.960
could have a devastating effect. Small islands like the one behind me could be totally submerged.

00:53:17.960 --> 00:53:24.840
Major cities could be at risk and the reason for that lies far away from here, where the

00:53:24.840 --> 00:53:36.520
change is already beginning to be seen at the poles. I am at the very centre of the

00:53:36.520 --> 00:53:44.120
great white continent, Antarctica. The South Pole is about half a mile away. For a thousand

00:53:44.120 --> 00:53:54.000
miles in all directions, there is nothing but ice. This white wilderness, this emptiness

00:53:54.000 --> 00:54:04.080
is the North Pole. I'm standing in the middle of a frozen ocean. I have been lucky enough

00:54:04.080 --> 00:54:09.640
to travel in the polar regions several times in the last 30 years, making films about their

00:54:09.640 --> 00:54:16.160
rich wildlife. His sole object in life at the moment is to make quite sure that he and

00:54:16.160 --> 00:54:23.680
he alone mates with every single one of them and so that he must fight. It's heavier even

00:54:23.680 --> 00:54:34.080
than, heavier than the adult. These parent birds reunite once they come back here onto

00:54:34.080 --> 00:54:42.280
their own patch of shingle. But although the Antarctic is virtually lifeless over vast areas,

00:54:42.280 --> 00:54:48.880
there are one or two smaller ways that team would like. Slowly I began to realise that

00:54:48.880 --> 00:54:53.760
things were changing in ways that will affect the wildlife and eventually ourselves, no

00:54:53.760 --> 00:55:11.320
matter how far away from the poles we might be. This is the ice that covered the Arctic

00:55:11.320 --> 00:55:19.440
Ocean in September 1980. Since then, there has been a 30% reduction in the area covered

00:55:19.440 --> 00:55:28.680
by ice. And not only that, what ice remains is only half as thick as it was. If the sea

00:55:28.680 --> 00:55:33.680
ice continues to melt at this rate, there will be open ocean in the summer at the North

00:55:33.680 --> 00:55:45.160
Pole within decades. The very whiteness of the snow and ice contributes to the pace of

00:55:45.160 --> 00:55:54.280
change. Light bouncing off it takes 90% of the sun's energy back into space. And this

00:55:54.280 --> 00:56:02.200
has helped to keep the planet cool. But when the sea ice melts, it exposes the dark sea

00:56:02.200 --> 00:56:07.800
water. That doesn't reflect the sun's heat, it absorbs it, so the temperature of the sea

00:56:07.800 --> 00:56:18.360
rises. Here in the Arctic, the climate is warming twice as fast as the rest of the Earth.

00:56:18.360 --> 00:56:35.360
And that could have global consequences, including rises in sea level around the world. Climate

00:56:35.360 --> 00:56:48.880
change is already affecting the lives of not only wild animals, but ourselves all over

00:56:48.880 --> 00:56:55.680
the globe.

00:56:55.680 --> 00:57:01.720
I've spent my life filming the natural world, and I've travelled to some pretty remote and

00:57:01.720 --> 00:57:09.100
exciting places in order to do so. I've enjoyed every minute of it. But every journey seems

00:57:09.100 --> 00:57:17.680
to have got quicker and shorter. It's as though the world has shrunk. But then, sadly, so

00:57:17.680 --> 00:57:25.680
have the wild places.

00:57:25.680 --> 00:57:31.800
The increasing size of the human population is having a devastating effect on the natural

00:57:31.800 --> 00:57:38.720
world. But fortunately, people are becoming aware of that and doing something about it.

00:57:38.720 --> 00:57:45.200
And I'd like to think that natural history films have helped in that process. And there

00:57:45.200 --> 00:57:51.200
are some signs of hope. Animals that I thought might become extinct in my lifetime are still

00:57:51.200 --> 00:57:57.600
with us and growing in numbers.

00:57:57.600 --> 00:58:03.440
We now have a better understanding of the natural world than ever. We know how best

00:58:03.440 --> 00:58:21.640
to protect it for future generations. I can only hope that we will.

